Date: Sun, 17 May 2026 18:28:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?Phase=203=20NH10:=20wrapper=20auto-terminate=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?am=5Frden=20for=20FAST=5FREAD=20=E2=80=94=20termination=20closu?=
 =?UTF-8?q?re=20on=20silicon?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the FAST_READ termination bug observed after NH9: epcs_ctrl
megafunction goes through stage4 fine and shifts data bytes (Phase
3 READ data closure already achieved per NH9), but after the read
loop the rden=0 pulse from firmware CTRL.b7 misses the
data_valid_wire pulse window so end_read_reg never fires, busy_wire
stays at 1 forever, fast_read_reg can't update (clk_en gated on
~busy_wire), and the whole megafunction is stuck until POR.

Root cause (verified 2026-05-17 night HW test on bitstream
d7c1950935ec3e815ce036f1d8d790e7, 3/3 deterministic C4 TIMEOUT):
- end_read_reg <= ((~rden) & (do_read|do_fast_read) & data_valid_wire
  & end_read_byte) per epcs_ctrl.v line 826
- data_valid_wire = dvalid_reg = (end_read_byte & end_one_cyc_pos)
  ena=do_read|do_fast_read, sclr=(end_op_wire|end_operation).  It's a
  SINGLE-CYCLE PULSE per byte shifted, not level-locked.
- firmware's CTRL.b7=RDEN_DROP is a single-cycle pulse via
  ctrl_rden_drop auto-clear.  am_rden = ~ctrl_rden_drop = 0 for 1
  cycle.
- Two single-cycle pulses must align in the same sysclk cycle for
  end_read_reg to fire.  Empirically impossible from firmware-side
  Wishbone writes (no cycle-accurate control over altasmi's internal
  FSM phase).  Hence 3/3 TIMEOUT.

NH10 FIX (wb_altasmi_parallel.v, 1-line change to am_rden):
  - wire am_rden = ~ctrl_rden_drop;
  + wire am_rden = ~ctrl_rden_drop & ~(am_busy & ~ctrl_fast_read);

Reading: am_rden=1 unless (firmware pulsed RDEN_DROP) OR (megafunction
is busy AND firmware has cleared the FAST_READ level bit).  The new
second term implements AUTO-TERMINATE: when firmware writes CTRL=0
while the FSM is still busy, am_rden is held LOW until busy drops.
end_read_reg gets unlimited cycles to align with the next
data_valid_wire pulse (~150 sysclks max) and fires reliably.

Firmware API unchanged.  The existing CTRL.b7 pulse path is preserved
(for back-compat) but is now redundant — firmware's
"*CTRL = CTRL_FAST_READ | CTRL_RDEN_DROP; wait_not_busy(); *CTRL = 0;"
and "*CTRL = 0; wait_not_busy();" both work identically.  The
epcs_fast_read() driver in epcs.c is unchanged; the diag firmware
in main.c is unchanged.

NH10 DIAG ENHANCEMENTS (sw/stage2_loader/main.c, +428 B ELF):
- Phase A enriched with per-iter ESTAT sampling (was just STATUS)
  to discriminate the originally-reported "Phase A regression".  3/3
  HW tests post-enhancement show Phase A actually completes at
  i=0x2 OK (consistent with NH8 baseline i=0x6); the one-off
  TIMEOUT observed in the first NH9 HW test (commit 8f3929e body)
  was a cold-boot one-off transient, NOT a real regression.
- A2 iter cap reduced 10M→1M for faster diag turnaround.
- A5/A6 added: empirical test of the "delay-then-read" workaround
  pattern.  256-cycle nop + ESTAT read shows valid status without
  needing wait_not_busy().  Confirmed working but not needed since
  Phase A wait_not_busy is itself working (with the enriched
  instrumentation).

HW-VERIFIED (capture md5 88548dd4 at orchestrator
docs/captures/diag_nh10_auto_terminate_ok.log):
  C1 after CTRL=FAST_READ STATUS=0x03         ← FAST_READ launched
  C2 byte=0..3 wait_dv i=0 STATUS=0x03 DATAOUT=0x21,0x00,0x88,0x00
  C3 after CTRL=FAST_READ|RDEN_DROP STATUS=0x03  ← legacy pulse,
                                                   still race-prone
  C4 post_rden_drop_wait TIMEOUT                 ← legacy pulse misses
                                                   dvalid window
  C5 after CTRL=0 STATUS=0x02                    ← AUTO-TERMINATE:
                                                   busy DROPPED to 0
                                                   (only DV bit left)

Compare prior NH9 run (no auto-terminate): C5 STATUS=0x03 stuck
forever.  3/3 deterministic across re-runs.

BUDGET (NH10):
  LE       8929 / 10320 (87 %, +49 from NH9's 8880)
  Slack    -1.182 ns setup CLOCK (worse than NH9 -1.003 by 0.18 ns;
           the new combinational term in am_rden shrinks routing
           margin; design has worked at negative slack throughout)
  RBF      md5 43f8578e0abf8e03bfba36c69f3b3edc (368011 B)
  ELF      16708 B (unchanged from NH9 + Phase A enrichment)

PHASE 3 STATUS POST-NH10:
- READ path: ✅ silicon-closed (epcs_fast_read returns real bytes)
- READ termination: ✅ silicon-closed (busy reliably drops after CTRL=0)
- RDSR path: ✅ working (epcs_read_status returns valid ESTAT)
- ERASE / PROGRAM: not yet HW-validated, but no longer blocked by
  termination — sequential read-then-write is now safe.

KNOWN CARRYOVER:
- Phase B diag (b0=READ) still no-op post-NH9 (CTRL.b0 dropped by
  wrapper); kept as control experiment.
- diag firmware Phase C still pulses RDEN_DROP (C3) before CTRL=0
  (C5).  C3 pulse misses dvalid window (TIMEOUT) but is harmless;
  C5 auto-terminate is what actually fires.  Firmware unchanged.
- Phase C bytes differ between bitstreams (5145a6d7: 0x7d 0x21 0x0f
  0x00; 43f8578e: 0x21 0x00 0x88 0x00).  Need investigation — may be
  off-by-one alignment or dummy-byte-vs-first-byte confusion.
  Doesn't block READ closure since bytes are non-0xff (real flash
  content) but the actual addr-0 content is one of these (or
  neither).  Follow-up.
---
 rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v | 20 +++++++----
 sw/stage2_loader/main.c   | 71 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 74 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
index 66bbba0..fb75812 100644
--- a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
+++ b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
@@ -125,13 +125,19 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
     // drop rden=0 for one cycle AFTER the final byte's data_valid
     // pulse to signal "this was the last byte → terminate read."
     //
-    // Default am_rden=1 (matches original behavior except now
-    // controllable).  Firmware pulses CTRL.b7=1 to drop rden for one
-    // cycle after consuming the last DATAOUT byte:
-    //   *CTRL = CTRL_READ | CTRL_RDEN_DROP;  // rden drops, end_read fires
-    //   wait_not_busy();                      // FSM completes
-    //   *CTRL = 0;                            // deassert READ
-    wire am_rden = ~ctrl_rden_drop;
+    // Default am_rden=1.  Two termination paths, both supported:
+    //   (a) Firmware pulses CTRL.b7=1 (single-cycle RDEN_DROP).  Race-prone:
+    //       the rden=0 pulse must align with the megafunction's single-cycle
+    //       data_valid_wire pulse for end_read_reg to fire.  Empirically
+    //       unreliable on NH9 (C4 TIMEOUT 3/3 reproducible).
+    //   (b) NH10 AUTO-TERMINATE: when firmware clears the FAST_READ level
+    //       bit (CTRL=0) while the megafunction is still busy, the wrapper
+    //       HOLDS am_rden=0 until busy drops.  end_read_reg gets unlimited
+    //       cycles to align with the data_valid_wire pulse — termination
+    //       always completes within one shift cycle (~150 sysclks).
+    //   Firmware API: just write CTRL=0 then wait_not_busy().  The b7 pulse
+    //   is retained for back-compat but is now redundant.
+    wire am_rden = ~ctrl_rden_drop & ~(am_busy & ~ctrl_fast_read);
 
     epcs_ctrl u_am (
         .clkin         (clk),
diff --git a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
index 6e29d87..27304fa 100644
--- a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
+++ b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
@@ -321,20 +321,25 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
     uart_puts("\r\n");
 
     {
-        /* bounded wait_not_busy on RDSR.  Iter cap empirically ~10M
-         * (NEORV32 @ 50 MHz, Wishbone read ~6 cycles → ~0.12 µs per
-         * iter → 10M ≈ 1.2 s).  Print every ~1M iters. */
+        /* NH9 PROBE v2 — bounded wait_not_busy with PERIODIC ESTAT sampling.
+         * If ESTAT changes across iterations, that confirms the
+         * auto-restart hypothesis (megafunction repeatedly completes
+         * RDSR while CTRL.b3 held high, updating statreg_int each cycle).
+         * Iter cap reduced 10M→1M (~120ms) for faster diag turnaround. */
         uint32_t i;
         uint32_t s = 0;
         uint32_t prints = 0;
-        for (i = 0; i < 10000000u; i++) {
+        for (i = 0; i < 1000000u; i++) {
             s = *DP_STATUS;
             if (!(s & 0x1u)) break;
-            if ((i & 0xFFFFFu) == 0u && prints < 12u) {
-                uart_puts("[P] A2 wait_not_busy i=");
+            if ((i & 0x1FFFFu) == 0u && prints < 8u) {
+                uint32_t e = *DP_ESTAT;
+                uart_puts("[P] A2 wait i=");
                 uart_puthex32(i);
                 uart_puts(" STATUS=");
                 uart_puthex32(s);
+                uart_puts(" ESTAT=");
+                uart_puthex32(e);
                 uart_puts("\r\n");
                 prints++;
             }
@@ -343,7 +348,7 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
         uart_puthex32(i);
         uart_puts(" final=");
         uart_puthex32(s);
-        uart_puts((i >= 10000000u) ? " TIMEOUT\r\n" : " OK\r\n");
+        uart_puts((i >= 1000000u) ? " TIMEOUT\r\n" : " OK\r\n");
     }
 
     uint32_t rdsr_byte = *DP_ESTAT;
@@ -351,10 +356,56 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
     uart_puthex32(rdsr_byte);
     uart_puts("\r\n");
 
+    /* A4 — measure how fast busy drops once CTRL=0 releases the op. */
     *DP_CTRL = 0;
-    uart_puts("[P] A4 after CTRL=0 STATUS=");
-    uart_puthex32(*DP_STATUS);
-    uart_puts("\r\n");
+    {
+        uint32_t i;
+        uint32_t s = 0;
+        for (i = 0; i < 10000u; i++) {
+            s = *DP_STATUS;
+            if (!(s & 0x1u)) break;
+        }
+        uart_puts("[P] A4 after CTRL=0 release i=");
+        uart_puthex32(i);
+        uart_puts(" STATUS=");
+        uart_puthex32(s);
+        uart_puts((i >= 10000u) ? " TIMEOUT\r\n" : " OK\r\n");
+    }
+
+    /* A5/A6 — workaround test: skip wait_not_busy, use fixed delay.
+     * If this returns a valid ESTAT (matching what A3 showed), the
+     * "delay-then-read-then-release" pattern is the firmware-side fix. */
+    uart_puts("[P] --- A2X: RDSR workaround (delay-then-read) ---\r\n");
+    *DP_CTRL = DC_RD_STATUS;
+    {
+        /* ~256 NOPs ≈ 256 sysclks ≈ 5 µs @ 50 MHz — should be ≥1 RDSR
+         * cycle (~150 sysclks per altasmi SPI byte transfer). */
+        volatile uint32_t spin;
+        for (spin = 0; spin < 256u; spin++) { }
+    }
+    {
+        uint32_t s_mid = *DP_STATUS;
+        uint32_t e_mid = *DP_ESTAT;
+        uart_puts("[P] A5 after ~256-cycle delay STATUS=");
+        uart_puthex32(s_mid);
+        uart_puts(" ESTAT=");
+        uart_puthex32(e_mid);
+        uart_puts("\r\n");
+    }
+    *DP_CTRL = 0;
+    {
+        uint32_t i;
+        uint32_t s = 0;
+        for (i = 0; i < 10000u; i++) {
+            s = *DP_STATUS;
+            if (!(s & 0x1u)) break;
+        }
+        uart_puts("[P] A6 workaround release i=");
+        uart_puthex32(i);
+        uart_puts(" STATUS=");
+        uart_puthex32(s);
+        uart_puts((i >= 10000u) ? " TIMEOUT\r\n" : " OK\r\n");
+    }
 
     /* =================================================================
      * Phase B — READ 1 byte @ 0x000000 (the prior failing path)
